<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amadu Seku</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1853">1853</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ahmadu II of Massina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amadu Seku was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a> and the second ruler of the Massina Empire, the Fulbe Islamic state of the inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> delta, from 1845 to 1853. He kept up his father's religious and administrative order based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdullahi">Hamdullahi</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadu-amadu">Amadu Amadu</a> succeeded him and was overthrown by Umar Tall in 1862.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amadu Seku, Amadu Amadu Hammadi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fula</a>, was born around 1815, the eldest son of Seku Amadu Lobbo of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a> family. Some time between 1810 and 1818 his father had launched a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Massina</a>, the inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> delta, against the Fulbe chiefs who paid tribute to the Bambara of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Segu</a>, and built an empire reaching from just below Segu almost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>. It was ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdullahi">Hamdullahi</a> by a council of forty elders under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> interpretation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a>, with provincial governors who were mostly his father's kinsmen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a> died on 19 March 1845, the council chose his son as almami, though the office required only learning and piety, not birth in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a> family. Ba Lobbo, son of Seku Amadu's eldest brother, would have continued the family's tradition of succession through a collateral line, and others such as Alfa Nuhum Tayru and al-Hadjdj Mody Seydu were better qualified; Ba Lobbo became the main leader of the army instead.</p><p>At first Amadu had to suppress internal opposition and revolts by the Saro Bambara and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, who declared independence. Ba Lobbo defeated the Tuareg in a surprise attack near Lake Gossi early in 1846, and in 1847 the Kunta leader Sidi al-Bekkai persuaded Amadu to withdraw his garrison from Timbuktu while accepting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Massina</a> rule. His reign built on his father's success in settling the once nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fula</a> and in fixing grazing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transhumance">transhumance</a> rights in law, though he found it hard to keep up the old enthusiasm for strict Islamic rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Amadu was killed during a raid on the Bambara in February 1853. His tomb can still be seen beside his father's in the ruins of the palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdullahi">Hamdullahi</a>. His rule is remembered as a short time of relative peace and prosperity between the violent reigns of his father and his son. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadu-amadu">Amadu III</a>, elected according to his wishes, was never secure in his authority, and the empire lasted little more than forty years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amadu II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Amadu Seku</b> and grandson Amadu Amadu succeeded him.</blockquote><a href="/state/massina-empire">Massina Empire</a></li></ul></section>
